# Project Sand — instruction and test authoring

## instruction.md

Rewriting it in fresh words is **mandatory** — an unchanged or lightly
paraphrased instruction gets the task returned.

This is the solving agent's only written prompt. The agent may inspect the
environment source, while verifier and solution assets remain protected, so
the contract must be complete without disclosing protected details or assuming
the source is hidden.

Aim for **simple, specific, short**. Short paragraphs with line breaks, no wall
of text, no dumped config or stack traces.

- **Symptom only.** Describe what a user observes and nothing else. Never name
  a file, function, variable, signature, or algorithm; never give pseudocode;
  never name the bug or gesture at where it lives.
- **State the posted rules in English.** The app's own rules — scoring, limits,
  lifecycle, what each control promises — belong in the instruction as plain
  prose, because that is what makes the expected behavior precise without
  leaking the cause. The agent is told what the app promises, never why it
  breaks that promise.
- Be concrete enough to be testable: "the counter advances 1, 2, 3, 4 with no
  gaps", not "level progression should work". Name the inputs and the expected
  outputs.
- **No BROKEN labels anywhere.** Not in the instruction, not as a comment or
  marker in the shipped source, not in a filename or CSS class. Read the base
  source for giveaway comments before submitting — a planted `// BROKEN` or
  `// TODO: fix` collapses the whole task to a search.
- **Every instruction must include a UI/layout claim** that makes the app
  objectively better for its own domain — not decoration. Cut-off text,
  overlapping controls, an element escaping its container, unreadable
  contrast, a control stack with no spacing. "Move the button 2px left" is not
  a UI claim; bad → objectively good is.
- Reference the images in one brief sentence each, saying at a high level what
  is wrong or right in them: "As you can see, the game words trail off the
  screen." Not a paragraph, not a pixel description.
- **Do not write the model's diagnosis for it.** This is the single most common
  reason a package comes back as too easy. Never state how many defects there
  are, never number them, never give a per-defect heading, never hand over the
  arithmetic ("$512,000 − $320,000 should read $188,000"), and never enumerate
  which screens are affected. State the rule the app promises and that it is
  broken; let the agent find the count, the locations and the cause. The
  register to aim for is "a single press does not correspond to a single edit",
  not "pressing undo once removes two accounts instead of one".
- Map every claim to at least one test before submitting. A claim with no test
  behind it must be trimmed or reworded — and if a test is deleted late (for
  example because it reached into internals), go back and fix the claim it was
  covering.

A model of the register and the mix — behavior defects plus a layout defect,
plainly observed:

> When I play this game, after 3 points scored, the fourth and fifth points
> aren't added to the score. The bonus point isn't added when I press the space
> bar and the up key together, even though the instructions say it should.
> Lastly, the game screen is cut off, the buttons overlap, and the words run off
> the edge. As you can see, the game words trail off the screen.

## Difficulty comes from the defect

Ambiguity is not difficulty; it is a rejection. State the expected behavior
precisely and put the difficulty in the defects.

**Keep one coherent user problem while making its root causes orthogonal, and
spread them across backend and frontend.** Several independent defect families
serving the same product contract beat repeated manifestations of one shallow
bug, because a partial fix then leaves most of the suite red. Do not bolt on
unrelated product requirements merely to inflate difficulty. What real depth
looks like in practice:

- The fail site is not the fix site — the symptom surfaces in one layer, the
  cause lives in another.
- One route calling the wrong helper while its siblings call the right one.
- A lifecycle the UI ignores: draft versus published, archived versus active,
  optimistic state that never reconciles.
- Subtle invariants: off-by-one, boundary conditions, ordering, encoding, or a
  race with a deterministically observable outcome.
- A contract, type, or configuration change that must be reconciled across
  several legitimate call sites.
- A small feature that only integrates correctly if the existing architecture
  is understood.
- Objective UI/layout defects — front-end correctness is genuinely hard for
  agents and counts as a defect family in its own right.

Anti-patterns that read as one bug wearing four hats, and get graded that way:
every defect living in the same engine file, or the same wrong constant copied
across four call sites. Fixing one teaches the agent all the others.

The agent solving this can read the whole codebase instantly. Anything it can
find by searching for the symptom string is too easy.

Difficulty is investigative depth, not patch size: a small reference patch can
be valid when finding and validating it requires understanding the system.

## The verifier

**A thin suite is a rejection.** The platform enforces a hard gate — at least
**6 `[F2P]` and 2 `[P2P]`, 8 minimum, with no maximum** — and blocks submission
below it. Untagged tests count toward neither number and duplicate titles are
rejected outright. Treat the gate as the floor of the floor: aim well past it,
because a suite that merely clears 8 reads as thin. Padding is not coverage:
near-duplicate assertions, checks of constants or source text, and guards that
silently pass when their subject is missing are invalid. Every instruction
claim needs a test, plus edge cases, plus UI bounds, plus pass-to-pass
regressions.

- Keep the existing `PREAMBLE`, `boot()`, and the `[F2P]` / `[P2P]` title
  prefixes exactly — the harness parses them.
- Where the downloaded package schema contains `fail_to_pass` /
  `pass_to_pass` name lists, they must match runner output character for
  character. Modern Import templates omit those lists and use tagged parser
  output plus exact `task.toml` counts instead. Never invent a root config file.
- Fail-to-pass must fail on base and pass on fixed; pass-to-pass must pass on
  both. Failures must come from the planted bug, never from setup, timeouts, or
  a missing element that was never part of the defect.
- Every test must execute the user-visible code path it claims to cover.
  Checking that a file exists or that a string appears in source verifies
  nothing.
- Test behavior, not implementation. **Never read app internals** — debug hooks
  such as `window.__game` (`.L`, `.warp()`, `.pts`, `.speed`) get flagged by
  automated review as coupling the suite to internals. Never grep the source,
  assert on private helper names, require particular imports, or depend on
  internal call order. A correct solution structured differently must pass.
- Deterministic *outcomes*, not necessarily deterministic data. Randomised
  fixtures are fine — the official template's own seeder uses `Math.random` —
  provided the seeder records what it generated in `fixture.json` and every
  assertion is derived from that fixture instead of a hardcoded literal. What
  must never vary between runs is whether a test passes. No network calls, no
  shared mutable state, no dependence on test order.
- A container starts fresh for a run, but tests within that run share a
  process. Each test must establish and clean up its own state.
- Full-sentence test names, never truncated mid-phrase.
- Prefer returning a diagnostic object from `page.evaluate` and asserting with
  `toEqual`, so a failure shows what differed.
- Include at least one test that interleaves interactions (for example continue
  and restart alternating) so a partial fix cannot pass.

### Aim for frontier depth, not Harbor-green

Harbor going green proves the pack is wired correctly. It says nothing about
difficulty, and a task tuned only until the runner is happy lands too easy.

The bar to design against: an agent that half-fixes the task, or that collapses
several defects into one plausible-looking change, still leaves **most of the
fail-to-pass suite red**. Sanity-check this by hand — take the most likely
partial fix an agent would write, trace it through the suite, and count what
turns green. If a single edit clears most of the suite, the defects are not
orthogonal enough yet.

### Cover UI quality objectively

Assert bounds, not pixels — an element inside its container with a sane gap,
two controls that do not overlap, text that is not clipped:

```javascript
const g = document.querySelector('.hud-glass').getBoundingClientRect();
const s = document.getElementById('stage').getBoundingClientRect();
return {
  gapTop: Number((g.top - s.top).toFixed(1)),   // expect >= 4 and <= 40
  inside: g.bottom <= s.bottom + 0.5 && g.left >= s.left - 0.5 && g.right <= s.right + 0.5,
};
```

Pick bounds wide enough that any reasonable implementation passes and narrow
enough that the broken layout fails. The rule for every UI assertion: it must
not dock a valid solution, and it must not let an invalid one through.

## The screenshot standard

Automated QA checks the images, and weak ones return an otherwise-good task.

- `broken.png` must show the defect **in the pixels**, in the state the agent
  will find it, and must be embedded in `instruction.md`.
- `target.png` is **optional**. Ship it only if it earns its place, embed it too,
  and make it **measurably different** from the broken shot — near-identical
  pairs are auto-rejected. It must use the same scenario, viewport, and
  application state as the broken image so the change is directly comparable.
  Deleting a target that adds nothing is allowed.
- An image must carry information the prose does not. One that merely restates
  the text is a common return reason.
- Every visual problem described needs visible evidence. Add another broken
  image whenever one view cannot show the next distinct layout failure; do not
  pad the package with duplicate angles. Prefer defect sets where several
  failures are photographable, and check whether the queue workspace exposes
  extra screenshot slots before relying on them.
- The visual change must move from objectively defective to professionally
  shippable for the product's domain. Tiny cosmetic nudges do not qualify.

## Reference patch

It must remain the thing that makes the failing tests pass. If the bug or the
tests widen, widen the patch. Confirm by reading the fixed source — never
assume it already covers every symptom in the instruction. The patch has to
solve the behavior, not hardcode around the tests or bypass the verifier, and
`solution/solve.sh` must apply cleanly against the base commit. The fixed state
should be the most professional version of the application that can reasonably
be shipped, not merely the minimum pixels needed for a test.
